UrbanEnergy Insight — Login Roles, Features & Working Process

For your UrbanEnergy Insight project, I recommend having exactly 3 login roles. Each login will have its own dashboard, permissions, and features, but all three will work with the same central database.

The idea is simple:

Upload energy data → Analyze consumption → Identify high-consumption areas → Plan improvements → Generate reports.

1. Which Logins Will Be There?

There will be exactly these three primary logins:

No.	Login	Main Responsibility	Dashboard Name
1	Energy Analyst	Analyze energy consumption and identify unusual patterns	Energy Intelligence Center
2	Data Manager	Upload, validate, correct, and manage energy datasets	Energy Data Workspace
3	Energy Planner & Administrator	Plan improvements, track actions, and manage system access when authorized	Urban Planning & Control Center

Important: There will be no separate citizen login, guest login, or fourth administrator login. All three roles will use the same authentication system.

Login page design

The login page should look modern and professional, with a combination of dark and light colors rather than only green.

Background: Dark navy and deep blue.
Primary buttons: Cobalt blue.
Highlights: Violet, coral, teal, and amber.
Cards and forms: White or warm ivory.
Typography: Clean, modern fonts.
Images: A realistic urban skyline or city-energy background.

Users will enter their email and password. After successful authentication, the system will open the dashboard associated with their assigned role.

2. Login 1: Energy Analyst
Purpose

The Energy Analyst examines energy records to understand consumption patterns and identify areas that may need attention.

Features

1. Energy Overview Dashboard

Total energy consumption.
Average consumption.
Number of urban areas in the dataset.
High-consumption area count.
Summary of the latest imported data.

2. Energy Trends

Daily, monthly, or yearly trends, depending on the available data.
Line charts showing changes in consumption.
Comparisons between different periods.

3. Area Comparison

Compare energy consumption across urban areas.
Identify areas with higher or lower consumption.
View comparisons using charts and tables.

4. Urban Energy Map

Display urban areas on an interactive map.
Show consumption details when a user selects an area.
Highlight areas exceeding the configured consumption threshold.
Filter areas by consumption level.

The map must use verified geographic coordinates. If coordinates are unavailable, the application should explain that the map cannot display those records rather than inventing locations.

5. High-Consumption Detection

Identify areas whose consumption exceeds a defined threshold.
Show the observed consumption, comparison baseline, and reason for the alert.
Allow the analyst to inspect the underlying records.

High consumption should be treated as a signal for investigation, not automatic proof of energy waste.

6. Reports

Export energy summaries and comparison results to CSV.
View the period and dataset used to generate the report.
How this login works

Example:

The Data Manager uploads a residential energy dataset.
The application validates and saves the records.
The Energy Analyst opens the dashboard.
The dashboard retrieves the latest saved records.
Charts and area comparisons update using those records.
The system identifies areas that exceed the configured threshold.
The analyst reviews the results and exports a report.
The Energy Planner uses the findings to prioritize potential improvements.

Permission: The analyst can view and analyze authorized energy data but cannot manage user accounts or change system permissions.

3. Login 2: Data Manager
Purpose

The Data Manager ensures that energy data is properly uploaded, validated, and stored before it is used for analysis.

This login is important because incorrect or incomplete records can lead to misleading results.

Features

1. Data Dashboard

Total datasets.
Number of imported records.
Successful and failed imports.
Latest import history.

2. Upload Energy Dataset

Upload CSV files.
Preview records before importing.
Map uploaded column names to the required fields.
Check that required columns are present.

Example columns:

Field	Example
Record date	2026-09-01
Urban area	Area A
Energy consumption	425.5
Energy unit	kWh
Geographic latitude	Optional
Geographic longitude	Optional

The exact required fields and units should be defined consistently in the database and application.

3. Data Validation
The application should check for:

Missing required values.
Invalid dates.
Negative or non-numeric energy values.
Invalid units.
Duplicate records.
Incorrectly formatted coordinates, if provided.

4. Data Correction

Display invalid rows and explain the errors.
Allow authorized corrections.
Let the user review the corrected data before saving it.

The application must not silently invent missing values or discard invalid records without explaining what happened.

5. Dataset Management

View previously uploaded datasets.
Review dataset details.
See the number of valid and invalid records.
Correct or replace records where permitted.

6. Import History

Show the file name and import time.
Show how many records were accepted or rejected.
Display validation errors and import status.
How this login works

Example:

The Data Manager logs in.
They select Upload Dataset.
They choose a CSV file.
The application previews the file.
The Data Manager maps the columns.
The application validates the records.
The Data Manager reviews any errors.
Valid records are saved to the central database through a controlled import process.
The application confirms the actual import result.
The Energy Analyst's dashboard retrieves the updated data.

Permission: The Data Manager can upload and maintain authorized datasets but cannot manage user permissions or access administrator-only settings.

4. Login 3: Energy Planner & Administrator
Purpose

This login connects energy analysis with practical improvement planning.

It has two separate permission levels within the same primary role:

Energy Planner: Creates and tracks improvement plans.
Administrator: Performs additional system-management tasks when explicitly granted administrator permissions.

This does not create a fourth login type.

A. Energy Planner Features

1. Planning Dashboard

High-consumption areas requiring review.
Priority areas based on defined criteria.
Proposed and ongoing actions.
Overdue or completed tasks.

2. Priority Assessment

Review areas identified by the Energy Analyst.
Compare consumption levels and available supporting evidence.
Assign a priority such as High, Medium, or Low.
Record why the area was prioritized.

3. Recommendations
Display practical suggestions associated with the observed problem, such as:

Investigate unusually high consumption.
Review building energy-use patterns.
Assess potential energy-efficiency improvements.
Compare consumption before and after an intervention.

Recommendations should be based on available evidence. The application must not claim guaranteed energy or financial savings.

4. Action Planning
Create an improvement action with:

Action title.
Description.
Target urban area.
Priority.
Responsible person or team, if configured.
Start date and deadline.
Current status.

5. Progress Tracking
Allow the planner to update action statuses:

Proposed
Planned
In Progress
Completed
On Hold

The planner can also add milestones and record progress.

6. Planning Reports

Export prioritized areas and action plans to CSV.
Review planned versus completed actions.
Track overdue tasks and milestones.
How the Planner works

Example:

The Energy Analyst identifies an area with unusually high consumption.
The planner opens the priority assessment screen.
They review the area's consumption and supporting evidence.
They assign a priority.
They create an improvement action.
They set a deadline and milestones.
They update progress as work continues.
The application stores the plan in the database.
The planner can revisit the action after logging out and back in.
B. Administrator-Only Features

These features appear only when the logged-in user has explicit administrator permissions.

User Management: View, create, disable, or update authorized user accounts.
Permission Management: Assign roles and control access.
Audit Logs: Review important actions, such as imports and permission changes.
System Health: View basic application and database status.

A planner without administrator permissions must not be able to access these features, even by entering an administrator URL directly.

5. How All Three Logins Work Together

The most important part of UrbanEnergy Insight is that the three dashboards are connected.

10
Complete workflow

Step 1 — Data Manager

Uploads a residential energy CSV file.

⬇️

Step 2 — Application

Validates the file and stores accepted records in the central database.

⬇️

Step 3 — Energy Analyst

Views consumption charts, compares areas, and identifies high-consumption patterns.

⬇️

Step 4 — Energy Planner

Reviews the findings, sets priorities, and creates improvement actions.

⬇️

Step 5 — Administrator, when authorized

Manages users, permissions, and system oversight.

⬇️

Step 6 — Shared database

Stores datasets, analytics inputs, planning actions, milestones, and relevant audit records.

What does real-time updating mean here?

Suppose the Data Manager uploads 500 valid records.

After the import succeeds:

The analyst dashboard should retrieve the updated records.
Consumption totals and charts should reflect the new data.
Area comparisons should use the updated dataset.
High-consumption analysis should recalculate when appropriate.
The planner should be able to review the latest analytical findings.

The planner's saved actions must also remain available after refreshing the page or logging in again.

Important distinction: This is real-time or near-real-time application data updating. It does not mean the system receives live electricity readings from meters. Live energy monitoring requires a genuine data feed from an authorized energy source.

6. Database Structure

Use one central PostgreSQL database for all three logins.

Table	Purpose
users	User accounts, password hashes, roles, and permissions
urban_areas	Urban area names and verified geographic information
datasets	Uploaded dataset details
energy_records	Validated energy consumption records
validation_results	Validation issues and record-level errors
import_history	Import outcomes and timestamps
planning_actions	Improvement actions and their statuses
planning_milestones	Milestones associated with actions
audit_logs	Important user and administrative activities

All dashboards should use the same database, with server-side authorization determining which operations each user can perform.

Do not use browser local storage as a replacement for the actual database.

